      <Graphics centerX="350" centerY="600" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="AU4FS-4, SRI, caspi">&lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt;-methyl-&lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;/sup&gt;-aminocarboxypropyl-pseudouridine (m&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt;acp&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;psi;) is considered the most complex modification found in eukaryotic rRNA. The modified base is located at the tip of helix 31 of the 18S rRNA , and is strongly conserved in eukaryotes. The formation of this modified nucleotide requires three successive modification reactions involving one H/ACA snoRNP (snR35) and two enzymes (Nep1/Emg1 and Tsr3).</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="18S-N1mN3acp-pseudoU" />
